WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.500
Welcome. This class is about choosing
useful AI work without

2
00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:09.000
treating a model as magic. We will
separate the model

3
00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:13.500
from the complete system around it, match
capability patterns to

4
00:00:13.500 --> 00:00:18.000
appropriate tasks, distinguish assistance
from automation, and decide where human

5
00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:22.500
review must remain. You do not need to
know how

6
00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:27.000
to train a model. You do need to ask

7
00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:31.500
disciplined questions about the outcome,
the information used, the actions

8
00:00:31.500 --> 00:00:36.000
allowed, the people affected, and what
happens when the result

9
00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:40.500
is wrong. Those questions work across
providers, products, and model

10
00:00:40.500 --> 00:00:45.000
families.

11
00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:50.000
Artificial intelligence is a broad label
for computer systems that

12
00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:55.000
perform tasks associated with perception,
prediction, language, planning, or

13
00:00:55.000 --> 00:01:00.000
decision support. Machine learning is one
way to produce that

14
00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:05.000
behavior by learning patterns from
examples rather than programming every

15
00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:10.000
rule. A model is the learned component
that transforms inputs

16
00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:15.000
into outputs, but a product is never only
the model.

17
00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:20.000
The complete system also includes
instructions, input data, retrieval,

18
00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:25.000
software, tools, permissions, user
experience, monitoring, and people who own

19
00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:30.000
the result. NIST frames AI risk management
around products, services,

20
00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:35.000
systems, organizations, and affected
people, not around an isolated response.

21
00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:40.000
That wider view changes evaluation. Ask
what information enters, which

22
00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:45.000
component supplies it, what the model may
propose, what tools

23
00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:50.000
may act, which controls limit those
actions, how outcomes are

24
00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:55.000
measured, and who responds to failure. A
fluent answer is

25
00:01:55.000 --> 00:02:00.000
one observable output from that system. It
does not prove

26
00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:05.000
that the input was trustworthy, that a
tool succeeded, or

27
00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:10.000
that the workflow is safe.

28
00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:14.000
Consider a meeting-summary assistant. In
the first design, a person

29
00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:18.000
pastes approved notes, the model drafts a
summary, and the

30
00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:22.000
person edits it before sharing. In the
second design, software

31
00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:26.000
records every meeting, identifies
participants, stores transcripts, drafts

32
00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:30.000
decisions, sends messages, and updates a
project system automatically. Both

33
00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:34.000
may use the same language model, yet they
are not

34
00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:38.000
the same use case. The second system
collects more sensitive

35
00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:42.000
data, has more integrations, can affect
more people, and can

36
00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:46.000
act before a person reviews each output.
Its evaluation must

37
00:02:46.000 --> 00:02:50.000
include recording consent, access control,
retention, participant identification,

38
00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:54.000
tool authorization, error recovery, and
the correctness of each external

39
00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:58.000
update. This comparison shows why model
capability is only one

40
00:02:58.000 --> 00:03:02.000
row in a system decision. Changing the
model does not

41
00:03:02.000 --> 00:03:06.000
remove the need to control data or recover
from a

42
00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:10.000
mistaken action.

43
00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:15.000
A useful starting point is to name the
capability pattern

44
00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:20.000
before naming a product. Generative
systems can draft, summarize, explain,

45
00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:25.000
transform formats, extract structure,
classify examples, brainstorm

46
00:03:25.000 --> 00:03:30.000
alternatives, or reason over material
supplied in context. Other AI

47
00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:35.000
systems may rank, recommend, forecast,
detect patterns, transcribe audio,

48
00:03:35.000 --> 00:03:40.000
interpret images, or control a physical
process. The label AI

49
00:03:40.000 --> 00:03:45.000
does not tell you which capability is
present, which evidence

50
00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:50.000
it uses, or how reliable it is for your

51
00:03:50.000 --> 00:03:55.000
case. Match the pattern to a measurable
outcome. If the

52
00:03:55.000 --> 00:04:00.000
outcome is a shorter policy summary,
define which policy version

53
00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:05.000
is authoritative, which facts must remain,
who reviews the draft,

54
00:04:05.000 --> 00:04:10.000
and how omissions are detected. If the
outcome is classifying

55
00:04:10.000 --> 00:04:15.000
support requests, define the categories,
representative test cases, unacceptable

56
00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:20.000
errors, escalation path, and what happens
to uncertain cases. Good

57
00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:25.000
candidates have suitable data, observable
quality, bounded consequences, and

58
00:04:25.000 --> 00:04:30.000
controls that match those consequences. A
visually impressive demo without

59
00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:35.000
a defined outcome is not yet a use case.

60
00:04:35.000 --> 00:04:40.000
Choose one task you know well. State the
desired outcome

61
00:04:40.000 --> 00:04:45.000
in one sentence, then name the capability
pattern you would

62
00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:50.000
test. Add one piece of trustworthy input
and one observable

63
00:04:50.000 --> 00:04:55.000
quality signal. Avoid naming a provider
until those four items

64
00:04:55.000 --> 00:05:00.000
are clear. Pause the class while you
write.

65
00:06:40.000 --> 00:06:45.313
Next, separate assistance from automation.
An assistant proposes work for

66
00:06:45.313 --> 00:06:50.625
a person to inspect. An automated system
may act without

67
00:06:50.625 --> 00:06:55.938
a person reviewing every output. The
difference is authority, not

68
00:06:55.938 --> 00:07:01.250
vocabulary. A model drafting three private
alternatives is assistance when

69
00:07:01.250 --> 00:07:06.563
the learner chooses whether to use any of
them. The

70
00:07:06.563 --> 00:07:11.875
same model becomes part of automation when
software sends one

71
00:07:11.875 --> 00:07:17.188
alternative to customers, changes a
record, deploys code, or controls

72
00:07:17.188 --> 00:07:22.500
equipment. As authority increases,
evaluation must expand beyond answer

73
00:07:22.500 --> 00:07:27.813
quality. Test permissions, tool arguments,
identity, logging, rate limits, stop

74
00:07:27.813 --> 00:07:33.125
conditions, rollback, and recovery from
partial failure. Start with assistance

75
00:07:33.125 --> 00:07:38.438
when the task is unfamiliar, evidence is
incomplete, or errors

76
00:07:38.438 --> 00:07:43.750
are expensive. Consider limited automation
only after representative evaluation shows

77
00:07:43.750 --> 00:07:49.063
dependable behavior and the complete
system can observe, contain, and

78
00:07:49.063 --> 00:07:54.375
reverse actions. Some tasks should remain
unautomated because the evidence

79
00:07:54.375 --> 00:07:59.688
is insufficient, affected people need
meaningful review or appeal, or

80
00:07:59.688 --> 00:08:05.000
the harm cannot be acceptably bounded.

81
00:08:05.000 --> 00:08:10.000
Checkpoint. A system drafts an
account-suspension recommendation from

82
00:08:10.000 --> 00:08:15.000
supplied evidence, but an authorized
person must inspect the evidence

83
00:08:15.000 --> 00:08:20.000
and approve any change. Is this assistance
or full automation?

84
00:08:20.000 --> 00:08:25.000
Name the feature that determines your
answer, and identify one

85
00:08:25.000 --> 00:08:30.000
control that would still be required.

86
00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:03.500
A strong answer calls this assistance
because an authorized person

87
00:10:03.500 --> 00:10:07.000
retains the decision and action. That does
not make the

88
00:10:07.000 --> 00:10:10.500
system risk free. The reviewer needs the
underlying evidence, not

89
00:10:10.500 --> 00:10:14.000
only the model recommendation. Access must
be limited, the decision

90
00:10:14.000 --> 00:10:17.500
should be auditable, affected people may
need a correction or

91
00:10:17.500 --> 00:10:21.000
appeal path, and the system must not
quietly change the

92
00:10:21.000 --> 00:10:24.500
record before approval. If you called it
automation only because

93
00:10:24.500 --> 00:10:28.000
software produced the recommendation,
revise your rule: classify the workflow

94
00:10:28.000 --> 00:10:31.500
by who has authority to act and whether
meaningful review

95
00:10:31.500 --> 00:10:35.000
occurs before the consequence.

96
00:10:35.000 --> 00:10:40.333
Human accountability does not disappear
when an AI output is

97
00:10:40.333 --> 00:10:45.667
accurate. People and organizations choose
the purpose, acceptable risk, data,

98
00:10:45.667 --> 00:10:51.000
model, tools, evaluation method,
deployment boundary, and response to harm.

99
00:10:51.000 --> 00:10:56.333
A model cannot accept responsibility, hear
an appeal, authorize a

100
00:10:56.333 --> 00:11:01.667
policy exception, or repair an injured
relationship. High-impact uses therefore

101
00:11:01.667 --> 00:11:07.000
need named owners, documented review
criteria, accessible correction or appeal

102
00:11:07.000 --> 00:11:12.333
paths, and evidence that performance is
acceptable for the people

103
00:11:12.333 --> 00:11:17.667
and situations affected. Accountability
also means knowing when not to

104
00:11:17.667 --> 00:11:23.000
proceed. If representative evidence is
missing, privacy expectations cannot be

105
00:11:23.000 --> 00:11:28.333
met, the system cannot be stopped safely,
or no one

106
00:11:28.333 --> 00:11:33.667
can own recovery, the responsible release
mode may be no

107
00:11:33.667 --> 00:11:39.000
AI or a narrowly bounded experiment. The
goal is not

108
00:11:39.000 --> 00:11:44.333
to maximize automation. The goal is to
achieve a worthwhile

109
00:11:44.333 --> 00:11:49.667
outcome while keeping authority, evidence,
and consequences visible to the

110
00:11:49.667 --> 00:11:55.000
people responsible for them.

111
00:11:55.000 --> 00:11:59.643
Watch the triage method on two tasks. Task
one is

112
00:11:59.643 --> 00:12:04.286
drafting three event descriptions from
approved facts for an organizer

113
00:12:04.286 --> 00:12:08.929
to edit. The outcome is bounded, the input
is known,

114
00:12:08.929 --> 00:12:13.571
the draft is reversible, and review
happens before publication. A

115
00:12:13.571 --> 00:12:18.214
sensible first release is assistance with
factual and style checks.

116
00:12:18.214 --> 00:12:22.857
Task two is automatically denying a person
access to an

117
00:12:22.857 --> 00:12:27.500
essential service based on incomplete
notes. The consequence is high,

118
00:12:27.500 --> 00:12:32.143
the evidence is weak, affected people need
explanation and appeal,

119
00:12:32.143 --> 00:12:36.786
and the proposed action may violate policy
or law. The

120
00:12:36.786 --> 00:12:41.429
correct first release is not automation.
It may be no

121
00:12:41.429 --> 00:12:46.071
AI, or a carefully governed
decision-support experiment that cannot

122
00:12:46.071 --> 00:12:50.714
perform the denial. For each task, write a
verification method

123
00:12:50.714 --> 00:12:55.357
and a stop condition. This keeps the
release decision connected

124
00:12:55.357 --> 00:13:00.000
to evidence rather than enthusiasm.

125
00:13:00.000 --> 00:13:05.000
Now complete the use-case triage activity
with four tasks from

126
00:13:05.000 --> 00:13:10.000
work, school, home, or a community
setting. For each one,

127
00:13:10.000 --> 00:13:15.000
name the outcome, input data, capability
pattern, affected people, and

128
00:13:15.000 --> 00:13:20.000
consequence of error. Choose no AI,
reviewed assistance, approval-gated

129
00:13:20.000 --> 00:13:25.000
action, or monitored automation. Add one
verification method and one

130
00:13:25.000 --> 00:13:30.000
stop or rollback condition. Your strongest
submission will explain why

131
00:13:30.000 --> 00:13:35.000
the highest-risk row receives the
strongest human control. You may

132
00:13:35.000 --> 00:13:40.000
use the transcript and the static system
map while you

133
00:13:40.000 --> 00:13:45.000
work.

134
00:16:45.000 --> 00:16:48.571
When you are ready, begin the knowledge
check. You will

135
00:16:48.571 --> 00:16:52.143
identify the parts of a complete AI
system, choose a

136
00:16:52.143 --> 00:16:55.714
strong starting task, distinguish
assistance from automation, preserve human

137
00:16:55.714 --> 00:16:59.286
accountability, and use outcome, data,
affected people, consequences, and

138
00:16:59.286 --> 00:17:02.857
verification to evaluate a use case.
Review any section or

139
00:17:02.857 --> 00:17:06.429
the text-only class before submitting. The
assessment opens only when

140
00:17:06.429 --> 00:17:10.000
you choose Begin knowledge check.

141
00:17:10.000 --> 00:17:13.750
Keep one sentence: judge the complete
system, not the impressive

142
00:17:13.750 --> 00:17:17.500
response. Match authority and controls to
the consequence of being

143
00:17:17.500 --> 00:17:21.250
wrong, and keep an accountable person able
to review, stop,

144
00:17:21.250 --> 00:17:25.000
correct, and recover.
